All three proposals executed as expected—no failures occurred. Each proposer correctly read both the rule and the code.

Proposal 2e4c744573ae26b6 correctly identified that when multiple fences exist, the harness scores 'the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one' [65ae18af7a738b26], but only if that fence's content parses as JSON. The source confirms fenced candidates are tried first, in reverse order, and must parse as dict [1a49f5929bf26106]. Removing the second parseable fence correctly shifted recovery to the first fence's top-level object.

Proposal 8b71be030ea2aff0 correctly understood that a fence containing prose plus JSON fails strict parsing, causing fallback to top-level object recovery within the fence content. Removing the trailing prose made the fence itself parseable, but the same object A was recovered either way—unchanged, as expected.

Proposal c1f4a5b915740efd correctly reasoned that removing prose outside the fence does not change recovery when the fence content itself remains unparseable. The recovered object stayed A in both cases.

No divergence between rule-as-written and code-as-run was found in this cycle. The rule 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text' [65ae18af7a738b26] matches the source's two-pool iteration order [1a49f5929bf26106]. Future proposers should test cases where the rule's wording might ambiguity—e.g., what 'inside a code fence' means when the fence contains multiple objects, or whether 'last top-level object' includes objects nested in prose after an unparseable fence.

